args.summary:
Fixed the proxy credentials leak over redirect-to-proxy vulnerability (CVE-2026-6253).

**Root cause:** In `lib/http.c`, the function `Curl_http_output_auth()` was sending proxy authentication headers for CONNECT requests (proxy tunnels) without checking if the *current* proxy connection actually has credentials configured. When curl follows a redirect from HTTP to HTTPS with different proxies (one with credentials, one without), the credentials from the first proxy were incorrectly being sent to the second proxy.

**Fix:** Added a check for `conn->bits.proxy_user_passwd` in the proxy tunnel authentication condition (line 827 in lib/http.c). This ensures proxy authentication headers are only sent when the current proxy connection actually has user credentials configured.

**Change made in lib/http.c:**
```c
#ifndef CURL_DISABLE_PROXY
    /* Send proxy authentication header if needed */
    if(conn->bits.httpproxy &&
       conn->bits.proxy_user_passwd &&   // <-- ADDED THIS CHECK
       (conn->bits.tunnel_proxy == (curl_bit)proxytunnel)) {
      result = output_auth_headers(data, conn, authproxy, request, path, TRUE);
      if(result)
        return result;
    }
    else
#else
```

This minimal change ensures that when following a cross-scheme redirect to a different proxy without credentials, the previous proxy's credentials are not leaked. Both regression tests (test2009 and test2011) now pass, along with all 31 nearby tests.

